<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    IPlatformInfoPort over the host process itself: the operating system, the
    build, and the language the user reads. No vendor package, nothing but
    System.

    🔒 A1 — WHY THIS IS ITS OWN PROJECT AND NOT TWO FILES IN Ambient.System.
    That project's own header draws the line and names this case: "the bound is
    the dependency, not the word 'ambient' … Ambient ENVIRONMENT — a filesystem
    path, an environment variable, a locale, a device fact — is a different
    dependency with a different failure mode and gets its own project." A clock
    and an id generator cannot fail; a locale the runtime cannot resolve and a
    device the host declines to name both can, and they fail in ways a caller
    has to handle. Different failure mode, different project.

    client/, beside Platform.Godot: this satisfies a `23` §4.1 CLIENT port, and
    the two implementations of one concern belong in one lane where a reader
    comparing them finds both. Ambient.System sits under shared/ because ITS
    ports are `23` §4.3 shared ones, so that rule points here, not there.

    ⚠️ NOTHING COMPOSES THIS YET. No composition root names it; the only
    projects that reference it are Contract.Tests and Architecture.Tests. The
    shipping client is the Godot one, and its engine adapter cannot be put
    behind the port at all - calling it outside the runtime is a fatal
    AccessViolationException, measured, and recorded where the measurement
    lives, in Platform.Godot's GodotUserPaths. So this exists to satisfy
    `23` §5 A5 with a REAL implementation rather than a second fake, which is
    the same reason M5-01 shipped Cache.LocalFile before M7-09 consumed it.

    🔒 WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL, stated plainly because the alternative reading is
    "a fake with a csproj around it": `23` §5 A5 needs two implementations and
    steering S7 needs one of them to be real. The engine adapter cannot be that
    one — see this project's HostPlatformInfo remarks for the measurement — so
    without this project the port could only have been declared over a fake, or
    not declared at all. M5-01 shipped Cache.LocalFile for exactly this reason
    and the M7-09 row records it.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\SlayIdleRepeat.Application\SlayIdleRepeat.Application.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The version chain takes its two assemblies as arguments so that every arm,
    including the failure, can be reached by a test rather than argued about.
    That makes it internal rather than public: the arms are a test's business,
    not a caller's.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="SlayIdleRepeat.Contract.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
